Excellent choice. You have mastered Authentication (AuthN - "Who are you?").
Now we enter Authorization (AuthZ - "What are you allowed to do?").
We are going to create a VIP Room.
 * Normal Users: Can access the App.
 * Admins: Can access the Admin Dashboard.
 * The Rule: If a Normal User tries to enter the Admin Dashboard, they get kicked out (403 Forbidden), even if they are logged in!
[1. The Setup: "Enable Method Security"]
By default, Spring Security defines rules in the SecurityConfig file. But in big projects, that file gets messy.
It is much better to put the security rule directly on the Controller.
Step 1: Go to your SecurityConfig.java and add one annotation to the top.
@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
@EnableMethodSecurity // <--- NEW: This turns on the @PreAuthorize annotation
public class SecurityConfig {
    // ... rest of your code stays the same
}

[2. The Code: Creating the VIP Room]
Create a new controller called AdminController.java.
We will use the @PreAuthorize annotation to put a guard dog at the door.
package org.adesh.security1.Controller;

import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.security.access.prepost.PreAuthorize;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/admin")
public class AdminController {

    // ONLY users with 'ROLE_ADMIN' can enter.
    // If a 'ROLE_USER' tries, they get 403.
    @GetMapping
    @PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')") 
    public ResponseEntity<String> adminOnlyEndpoint() {
        return ResponseEntity.ok("Welcome, Boss! Only Admins can see this.");
    }
    
    // Both ADMIN and USER can enter this one
    @GetMapping("/public")
    @PreAuthorize("hasAnyRole('ADMIN', 'USER')")
    public ResponseEntity<String> everyoneEndpoint() {
        return ResponseEntity.ok("Hello! Everyone is welcome here.");
    }
}

[3. The Critical Rule: The "ROLE_" Prefix]
Spring Security is extremely strict about naming.
 * In your Database: The role MUST start with ROLE_. (e.g., ROLE_ADMIN, ROLE_USER).
 * In your Code: You write hasRole('ADMIN'). (Spring automatically adds the ROLE_ prefix for you).
Common Mistake:
 * DB: ADMIN \rightarrow Code: hasRole('ADMIN') \rightarrow FAIL (Spring looks for ROLE_ADMIN).
 * DB: ROLE_ADMIN \rightarrow Code: hasRole('ROLE_ADMIN') \rightarrow FAIL (Spring looks for ROLE_ROLE_ADMIN).
Correct Setup:
 * DB Value: ROLE_ADMIN
 * Annotation: hasRole('ADMIN')
[4. Testing Plan (Postman)]
Since we don't have an "Admin Registration" page, we have to cheat a little bit using the Database.
Step 1: Create a Normal User
 * Register: POST /api/auth/register with username "user1".
 * (This creates a user with default ROLE_USER).
Step 2: Create an Admin (The Hack)
 * Register: POST /api/auth/register with username "admin1".
 * Go to Database: Manually update the row for "admin1".
   * SQL: UPDATE users SET role = 'ROLE_ADMIN' WHERE username = 'admin1';
   * (Make sure your entity field is actually called 'role' or whatever you named it).
Step 3: The "Kick Out" Test
 * Login as "user1". Copy the Token.
 * Access GET /api/admin with user1's token.
 * Result: 403 Forbidden (Correct! The guard stopped you).
Step 4: The "VIP Access" Test
 * Login as "admin1". Copy the Token.
 * Access GET /api/admin with admin1's token.
 * Result: 200 OK - "Welcome Boss!" (Correct! The guard saw your rank).
